WEBVTT

00:00:00.088 --> 00:00:03.375
Imagine a client asking what this project
actually does.

00:00:03.776 --> 00:00:07.452
Computers send information in small units called
packets.

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:11.492
This project takes the recorded content of one
packet and gives

00:00:11.492 --> 00:00:15.431
it scores for two supplied categories: benign
and attack.

00:00:15.845 --> 00:00:18.994
Benign is the category used for ordinary traffic
in these

00:00:18.994 --> 00:00:19.672
examples.

00:00:20.073 --> 00:00:22.143
Attack is the other training category.

00:00:22.545 --> 00:00:25.970
The model learns patterns in numerical examples;
it does not

00:00:25.970 --> 00:00:29.244
investigate an incident or know the customer&#x27;s
business.

00:00:29.646 --> 00:00:32.519
Our job throughout this video is to connect that
simple

00:00:32.531 --> 00:00:36.383
description to the files, the trained model, and
the evidence

00:00:36.396 --> 00:00:37.939
behind its measured behavior.

00:00:38.463 --> 00:00:40.208
Three things play different roles here.

00:00:40.622 --> 00:00:44.174
The research paper explains a proposed method,
much as a recipe

00:00:44.174 --> 00:00:45.982
explains how a dish could be made.

00:00:46.396 --> 00:00:50.375
The repository contains the programs and
instructions used to put

00:00:50.375 --> 00:00:51.894
the method into practice.

00:00:52.308 --> 00:00:56.174
The evidence records what happened when those
programs were run.

00:00:56.576 --> 00:00:59.688
The two uploaded tables supply the packet
examples.

00:01:00.078 --> 00:01:03.805
They are not instructions, and the paper is not
fed into the

00:01:03.818 --> 00:01:04.634
classifier.

00:01:05.048 --> 00:01:08.789
When presenting a result to a client, connect
the claim to the

00:01:08.789 --> 00:01:09.943
matching evidence.

00:01:10.345 --> 00:01:14.588
A diagram explains an idea; a saved measurement
supports a claim

00:01:14.588 --> 00:01:16.295
about an executed experiment.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:20.557
Follow the same route whenever you explain or
run the client

00:01:20.557 --> 00:01:21.097
delivery.

00:01:21.498 --> 00:01:26.581
Start with both supplied packet files, the C I C
I D S twenty

00:01:26.581 --> 00:01:29.743
seventeen export and the U N S W export.

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:34.524
Preparation checks their contents and organizes
repeated packet

00:01:34.524 --> 00:01:35.202
payloads.

00:01:35.591 --> 00:01:38.904
Training then uses selected examples from both
sources.

00:01:39.306 --> 00:01:42.556
Validation chooses the saved version that will
be used for

00:01:42.556 --> 00:01:43.058
prediction.

00:01:43.472 --> 00:01:46.522
That chosen classifier is called the joint
pretrained model.

00:01:46.911 --> 00:01:51.039
Later, a separate unlabeled packet file goes
through that saved

00:01:51.052 --> 00:01:52.370
model to produce scores.

00:01:52.759 --> 00:01:56.737
Every step in this course belongs to that single
supported packet

00:01:56.737 --> 00:01:57.339
workflow.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:00.574
The model&#x27;s name tells you two useful facts.

00:02:00.988 --> 00:02:04.376
Joint means that its supervised training
includes examples from

00:02:04.376 --> 00:02:05.719
both uploaded sources.

00:02:06.108 --> 00:02:09.396
Pretrained means that the central encoder begins
with released

00:02:09.396 --> 00:02:10.802
weights learned earlier.

00:02:11.203 --> 00:02:15.332
An encoder turns input numbers into a
representation that later

00:02:15.345 --> 00:02:16.223
layers can use.

00:02:16.587 --> 00:02:19.674
A new final layer learns the two packet
categories.

00:02:20.089 --> 00:02:23.427
The released initialization file is not the
finished joint

00:02:23.427 --> 00:02:24.255
classifier.

00:02:24.669 --> 00:02:28.622
The repository also trained comparison models
and simpler

00:02:28.635 --> 00:02:31.070
controls to investigate this choice.

00:02:31.471 --> 00:02:33.994
Those experiments help explain the evidence.

00:02:34.408 --> 00:02:38.097
They do not give the client several competing
setup routes.

00:02:38.499 --> 00:02:42.289
For the supported demonstration and prediction
commands, we use

00:02:42.289 --> 00:02:45.489
the validation selected joint pretrained
classifier.

00:02:46.017 --> 00:02:48.513
Think of training as practice with an answer
key.

00:02:48.914 --> 00:02:51.624
The model makes a prediction, compares it with
the supplied

00:02:51.636 --> 00:02:54.395
answers, and adjusts numbers called weights.

00:02:54.797 --> 00:02:57.155
A checkpoint saves those learned numbers
together with

00:02:57.167 --> 00:02:59.186
information needed to interpret them.

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.774
Inference means using a selected checkpoint
while keeping its

00:03:02.786 --> 00:03:03.777
weights fixed.

00:03:04.178 --> 00:03:08.167
It resembles applying an established rule to
another example.

00:03:08.556 --> 00:03:11.954
During inference the packet bytes are available,
but the true

00:03:11.967 --> 00:03:13.497
answer need not be.

00:03:13.924 --> 00:03:17.649
Without a known answer, we can report a
prediction, but we cannot

00:03:17.649 --> 00:03:20.007
measure whether that prediction was correct.

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:23.368
Downloading source code alone does not supply a
trained

00:03:23.368 --> 00:03:23.945
checkpoint.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.865
There are two repositories because different
readers need

00:03:27.877 --> 00:03:28.856
different packages.

00:03:29.258 --> 00:03:33.387
Net Mamba Plus reproduction is the full
development, research,

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:34.730
and learning source.

00:03:35.119 --> 00:03:39.098
It includes the implementation, measured
evidence, explanations,

00:03:39.287 --> 00:03:40.266
and teaching media.

00:03:40.655 --> 00:03:45.261
Net Mamba Plus client is the concise executable
delivery, with

00:03:45.261 --> 00:03:49.328
setup instructions, packet programs, tests, and
the packet

00:03:49.328 --> 00:03:50.683
evidence needed for handoff.

00:03:51.085 --> 00:03:55.503
The client edition omits the teaching videos,
PowerPoint decks,

00:03:55.666 --> 00:03:56.771
and presenter scripts.

00:03:57.173 --> 00:03:59.620
Both describe the same packet model workflow.

00:04:00.047 --> 00:04:03.637
The client has separate Git history and receives
verified

00:04:03.649 --> 00:04:08.318
exports; its client manifest identifies the
source revision and

00:04:08.318 --> 00:04:09.285
delivered files.

00:04:09.674 --> 00:04:12.950
Send the client repository together with the
client project

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:13.352
guide.

00:04:13.766 --> 00:04:17.330
Use the reproduction repository when someone
wants to understand

00:04:17.330 --> 00:04:19.452
the broader development and teaching record.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:25.207
A useful client explanation separates a working
capability from

00:04:25.207 --> 00:04:26.437
the environment around it.

00:04:26.851 --> 00:04:29.937
This delivery contains runnable packet
classification,

00:04:30.338 --> 00:04:33.926
verification tools, and an offline viewer of
recorded

00:04:33.939 --> 00:04:34.641
predictions.

00:04:35.055 --> 00:04:38.468
The viewer helps inspect the experiment; opening
it does not

00:04:38.468 --> 00:04:41.466
capture traffic or run fresh model inference.

00:04:41.868 --> 00:04:45.619
The measured results describe selected examples
from the supplied

00:04:45.619 --> 00:04:46.309
exports.

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:50.512
They do not establish detection quality on every
real network.

00:04:50.926 --> 00:04:55.279
As we continue, keep following one question:
what enters each

00:04:55.279 --> 00:04:59.030
step, what leaves it, and which saved record
supports that

00:04:59.030 --> 00:04:59.620
description?

00:05:00.034 --> 00:05:03.785
That habit lets you explain the repository
confidently without

00:05:03.785 --> 00:05:05.629
needing to become its programmer.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:16.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Explain the packet
route and the purpose of each repository.

00:05:16.113 --> 00:05:19.673
The paper asks how a model can classify network
traffic while

00:05:19.673 --> 00:05:21.930
processing sequences efficiently.

00:05:22.331 --> 00:05:26.619
Its Net Mamba Plus representation combines three
views of related

00:05:26.631 --> 00:05:27.170
traffic.

00:05:27.584 --> 00:05:29.527
Bytes represent recorded content.

00:05:29.929 --> 00:05:32.800
Packet sizes describe how large the pieces are.

00:05:33.213 --> 00:05:35.708
Arrival intervals describe the gaps between
them.

00:05:36.109 --> 00:05:40.196
Imagine observing deliveries: what each package
contains, how

00:05:40.196 --> 00:05:43.443
large it is, and how deliveries are spaced can
provide different

00:05:43.443 --> 00:05:44.033
clues.

00:05:44.446 --> 00:05:48.496
The technical word multimodal means these
multiple traffic views.

00:05:48.909 --> 00:05:52.056
It does not mean a conversation involving
pictures and audio.

00:05:52.457 --> 00:05:55.830
Before copying an architecture diagram, we must
check which of

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:58.174
those observations our actual data can support.

00:05:58.634 --> 00:06:01.042
Mamba is a selective state space model.

00:06:01.456 --> 00:06:04.856
The name sounds difficult, but begin with a
simple picture:

00:06:05.458 --> 00:06:08.770
reading a sequence while continually updating a
small numerical

00:06:08.783 --> 00:06:09.360
notebook.

00:06:09.761 --> 00:06:11.091
The notebook is the state.

00:06:11.492 --> 00:06:15.444
Each new input influences how that state changes
and which

00:06:15.457 --> 00:06:16.924
information is carried forward.

00:06:17.326 --> 00:06:21.240
Selective means that this behavior depends on
the input, using

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:22.381
learned calculations.

00:06:22.795 --> 00:06:27.124
The notebook analogy explains information flow;
the actual state

00:06:27.224 --> 00:06:28.566
is a collection of numbers.

00:06:28.968 --> 00:06:32.744
It is neither written reasoning nor a live
connection database.

00:06:33.133 --> 00:06:36.457
In our project this encoder processes packet
byte

00:06:36.457 --> 00:06:40.459
representations, then passes useful numerical
summaries to a

00:06:40.459 --> 00:06:41.237
classifier.

00:06:41.713 --> 00:06:45.513
The paper&#x27;s flow representation groups related
packets according

00:06:45.526 --> 00:06:47.558
to connection and ordering rules.

00:06:47.972 --> 00:06:51.734
Picture sorting letters into conversations: you
need reliable

00:06:51.747 --> 00:06:55.673
information about who wrote to whom and which
message came first.

00:06:56.074 --> 00:07:00.564
The supplied packet tables lack the addresses,
ports, connection

00:07:00.564 --> 00:07:03.850
identifiers, and established ordering needed for
that

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:04.490
reconstruction.

00:07:04.929 --> 00:07:09.169
Five neighboring spreadsheet rows might describe
unrelated

00:07:09.169 --> 00:07:10.109
conversations.

00:07:10.498 --> 00:07:14.436
Matching their labels or protocol names does not
repair the

00:07:14.436 --> 00:07:15.402
missing information.

00:07:15.778 --> 00:07:18.588
Even the recorded time difference has an
undocumented

00:07:18.588 --> 00:07:19.403
interpretation.

00:07:19.805 --> 00:07:23.630
We therefore treat each row&#x27;s stored payload as
one packet

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:24.220
example.

00:07:24.634 --> 00:07:27.531
This explicit choice determines what the
resulting measurements

00:07:27.543 --> 00:07:27.995
can mean.

00:07:28.492 --> 00:07:32.253
Think of pretraining as practice with part of
each example

00:07:32.253 --> 00:07:32.529
hidden.

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:36.716
The model sees the remaining information and
tries to reconstruct

00:07:36.916 --> 00:07:37.481
what was hidden.

00:07:37.894 --> 00:07:41.730
Its answer key is the original input content,
rather than an

00:07:41.730 --> 00:07:42.733
attack category.

00:07:43.147 --> 00:07:46.582
The reconstruction error guides adjustments to
the weights.

00:07:46.971 --> 00:07:50.381
This can produce a useful starting
representation before a later

00:07:50.393 --> 00:07:52.487
classification task has been learned.

00:07:52.875 --> 00:07:56.185
Our packet model loads the encoder weights
released by the

00:07:56.185 --> 00:07:57.200
paper&#x27;s authors.

00:07:57.614 --> 00:08:00.698
We did not recreate their complete earlier
training corpus.

00:08:01.099 --> 00:08:05.224
The checkpoint&#x27;s full exposure history remains
unknown, so

00:08:05.236 --> 00:08:08.470
transferring those weights does not establish
that earlier

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:09.837
examples were independent.

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:14.288
Supervised classification asks a different
question from

00:08:14.301 --> 00:08:15.028
reconstruction.

00:08:15.417 --> 00:08:19.292
Instead of filling in missing input content, the
model learns

00:08:19.304 --> 00:08:22.000
which supplied category belongs with an example.

00:08:22.439 --> 00:08:24.897
Here those categories are benign and attack.

00:08:25.298 --> 00:08:28.697
The pretrained encoder provides a starting
point, and a fresh

00:08:28.709 --> 00:08:31.681
classification head learns how to turn its
summaries into two

00:08:31.694 --> 00:08:32.296
scores.

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:35.168
A head is simply the final decision layer.

00:08:35.569 --> 00:08:39.030
During training the known labels guide weight
changes; they are

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:41.563
not inserted into the encoder&#x27;s input.

00:08:41.952 --> 00:08:44.861
This distinction matters when explaining the
research connection:

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:49.238
we reuse the learning machinery while adapting
its observation

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:51.783
unit and its final classification task.

00:08:52.254 --> 00:08:55.865
In the packet adaptation, the actual
observations are the fifteen

00:08:55.865 --> 00:08:57.557
hundred stored payload bytes.

00:08:57.971 --> 00:09:02.045
The size and timing branches contain no observed
sequences.

00:09:02.459 --> 00:09:05.605
Two learned prefix components remain, but they
are model

00:09:05.618 --> 00:09:08.702
parameters, not measurements taken from the
spreadsheet.

00:09:09.103 --> 00:09:13.929
The recorded time to live, length, protocol,
time difference,

00:09:14.017 --> 00:09:18.104
dataset identity, and label never enter the
neural forward pass,

00:09:18.542 --> 00:09:20.811
meaning the calculation that produces its
scores.

00:09:21.213 --> 00:09:25.926
Metadata is examined separately and used in
diagnostic controls.

00:09:26.327 --> 00:09:30.025
This is why a drawing with three traffic views
needs a careful

00:09:30.025 --> 00:09:33.485
caption: it describes the paper&#x27;s architecture,
while this

00:09:33.485 --> 00:09:36.394
adaptation supplies only the packet bytes.

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:40.566
The paper reports ninety seven point five zero
percent flow

00:09:40.704 --> 00:09:46.009
accuracy on C I C I o T twenty twenty two in
Table Four.

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:51.978
That dataset is different from the uploaded C I
C I D S twenty

00:09:51.978 --> 00:09:52.969
seventeen export.

00:09:53.383 --> 00:09:56.807
Our packet study measures a different
observation unit, different

00:09:56.807 --> 00:10:00.895
data, a binary target, and group weighted
balanced accuracy.

00:10:01.296 --> 00:10:05.221
Balanced accuracy averages how well the two
categories are

00:10:05.221 --> 00:10:05.773
recovered.

00:10:06.137 --> 00:10:09.711
Ordinary accuracy counts the overall fraction
correct.

00:10:10.112 --> 00:10:13.222
Similar percentages do not make those
measurements

00:10:13.235 --> 00:10:14.012
interchangeable.

00:10:14.414 --> 00:10:18.264
The repository has not reproduced the paper&#x27;s
flow result through

00:10:18.276 --> 00:10:19.417
these CSVs.

00:10:19.819 --> 00:10:23.267
When a client asks whether the adaptation
matches the paper,

00:10:23.669 --> 00:10:25.675
explain the changed task first.

00:10:26.089 --> 00:10:30.340
Likewise, the paper&#x27;s efficiency or deployment
claims do not

00:10:30.403 --> 00:10:33.626
automatically describe our checked GPU
execution.

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:44.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Why is this a
packet adaptation rather than the paper&#x27;s flow
experiment?

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:48.240
Now open the first source conceptually, as
though we were looking

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:50.485
across a very wide spreadsheet.

00:10:50.874 --> 00:10:55.766
C S V means comma separated values, a text
format containing rows

00:10:55.779 --> 00:10:56.519
and columns.

00:10:56.908 --> 00:11:03.067
This supplied C I C I D S twenty seventeen
export contains one

00:11:03.067 --> 00:11:06.980
million, four hundred ten thousand, two hundred
fifty five packet

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:09.389
rows and fifteen original labels.

00:11:09.778 --> 00:11:11.985
Each row is one stored packet example.

00:11:12.387 --> 00:11:16.602
The row count does not count connections,
distinct payloads, or

00:11:16.614 --> 00:11:17.868
successful predictions.

00:11:18.282 --> 00:11:22.949
The dataset name identifies the supplied export
under study; it

00:11:22.961 --> 00:11:26.862
does not establish every detail of how that
export was originally

00:11:26.862 --> 00:11:27.465
produced.

00:11:27.921 --> 00:11:30.204
Move across a single row from left to right.

00:11:30.605 --> 00:11:34.017
The first fifteen hundred columns are ordered
payload byte slots.

00:11:34.418 --> 00:11:38.105
They are followed by four metadata fields and
then the label,

00:11:38.394 --> 00:11:41.115
giving fifteen hundred and five columns
altogether.

00:11:41.517 --> 00:11:44.828
Metadata means recorded information about the
example.

00:11:45.229 --> 00:11:47.048
The label is its supplied answer.

00:11:47.449 --> 00:11:50.271
Keep these three roles separate even though the
spreadsheet

00:11:50.283 --> 00:11:51.814
places them side by side.

00:11:52.227 --> 00:11:55.865
The preparation code expects the exact header
sequence, so

00:11:55.865 --> 00:11:58.724
swapping columns changes the input contract.

00:11:59.188 --> 00:12:03.177
Fifteen hundred byte columns describe positions
within one packet

00:12:03.177 --> 00:12:06.613
representation; they are not fifteen hundred
independent packets

00:12:06.826 --> 00:12:08.482
or separate training examples.

00:12:08.954 --> 00:12:12.391
A byte is a small numerical value from zero
through two hundred

00:12:12.391 --> 00:12:13.169
fifty five.

00:12:13.558 --> 00:12:18.073
In the CSV it is written as text, and
preparation checks that it

00:12:18.086 --> 00:12:21.284
represents an exact valid integer before
conversion.

00:12:21.748 --> 00:12:24.294
Think of an ordered strip of numbered tiles.

00:12:24.708 --> 00:12:28.107
The model receives the strip&#x27;s numerical
pattern; this is not a

00:12:28.107 --> 00:12:31.431
promise to translate its contents into readable
messages.

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:34.780
A tile containing zero is still part of the
stored

00:12:34.780 --> 00:12:35.608
representation.

00:12:35.997 --> 00:12:39.772
The export does not reliably identify which
zeros are padding.

00:12:40.186 --> 00:12:43.673
We preserve all the stored slots, rather than
guessing that a

00:12:43.673 --> 00:12:47.197
zero or recorded length justifies removing them.

00:12:47.667 --> 00:12:50.114
The next fields describe recorded properties.

00:12:50.516 --> 00:12:54.958
Time to live relates to a packet&#x27;s lifetime
across the network.

00:12:55.359 --> 00:12:59.362
Total length records a packet length, but does
not identify

00:12:59.639 --> 00:13:01.282
meaningful payload slots.

00:13:01.697 --> 00:13:06.528
Protocol records a carrying category such as T C
P or U D P;

00:13:06.967 --> 00:13:08.297
it is not an attack answer.

00:13:08.699 --> 00:13:12.903
Time delta records a difference whose units and
reference event

00:13:13.066 --> 00:13:14.471
are not established here.

00:13:14.873 --> 00:13:18.211
These values are profiled, and the separate
metadata controls

00:13:18.211 --> 00:13:19.566
validate and use them.

00:13:19.967 --> 00:13:22.628
They never enter the native packet classifier.

00:13:23.029 --> 00:13:26.468
None supplies the missing connection identity or
proves how

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:28.413
neighboring rows should be ordered.

00:13:28.867 --> 00:13:31.402
The last column supplies the example&#x27;s label.

00:13:31.803 --> 00:13:36.145
In this source, the registered label benign maps
to class zero.

00:13:36.546 --> 00:13:41.515
The other explicitly registered labels map to
class one, attack.

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:45.405
Unknown spellings are rejected instead of
silently becoming an

00:13:45.405 --> 00:13:46.397
attack example.

00:13:46.798 --> 00:13:50.977
During supervised training, these answers guide
learning after

00:13:50.977 --> 00:13:52.658
the model produces its scores.

00:13:53.047 --> 00:13:55.933
Giving the answer to the model as an input would
defeat the

00:13:55.933 --> 00:13:59.710
purpose, like leaving the solution printed on an
exam question.

00:14:00.124 --> 00:14:03.173
Later, known labels can score held out
predictions.

00:14:03.575 --> 00:14:07.603
The separate prediction command requires an
unlabeled input, so

00:14:07.603 --> 00:14:10.200
its model must decide from the stored bytes
alone.

00:14:10.642 --> 00:14:14.254
A large spreadsheet can repeat the same input
many times.

00:14:14.642 --> 00:14:18.643
In this source, the original rows reduce to four
hundred thirty

00:14:18.655 --> 00:14:22.857
eight thousand, eight hundred eighty six
distinct stored payload

00:14:22.857 --> 00:14:23.346
groups.

00:14:23.747 --> 00:14:27.472
A group collects rows whose entire payload byte
sequence is

00:14:27.472 --> 00:14:28.073
identical.

00:14:28.475 --> 00:14:32.877
Imagine receiving repeated photocopies of the
same page: counting

00:14:32.889 --> 00:14:36.689
copies and counting distinct pages answer
different questions.

00:14:37.090 --> 00:14:40.677
Preparation keeps the original label counts
within each group,

00:14:41.078 --> 00:14:42.495
including disagreements.

00:14:42.897 --> 00:14:46.609
It does not pretend that repeated rows supply
the same diversity

00:14:46.947 --> 00:14:48.088
as new payloads.

00:14:48.502 --> 00:14:52.239
This distinction will matter both when dividing
the data and when

00:14:52.239 --> 00:14:54.171
interpreting a model&#x27;s reported score.

00:14:54.634 --> 00:14:58.474
Support means how many examples actually stand
behind a

00:14:58.486 --> 00:14:59.051
measurement.

00:14:59.465 --> 00:15:03.117
The export has fifteen original categories, but
that does not

00:15:03.130 --> 00:15:05.840
guarantee useful test coverage for every one.

00:15:06.254 --> 00:15:09.806
After duplicate grouping and the study&#x27;s
selection limits, the

00:15:09.806 --> 00:15:15.014
selected C I C test set contains no port scan
rows and only four

00:15:15.026 --> 00:15:17.122
distributed denial of service rows.

00:15:17.536 --> 00:15:20.862
We therefore have no measured port scan
performance there, and

00:15:20.862 --> 00:15:24.978
four rows cannot support a broad claim about
distributed attacks.

00:15:25.392 --> 00:15:28.793
Preparation accounts for the whole file, while
training uses

00:15:28.793 --> 00:15:30.650
selected and sampled groups.

00:15:31.064 --> 00:15:34.578
Always ask what reached the relevant experiment,
not just what

00:15:34.578 --> 00:15:35.632
arrived originally.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:46.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Explain the three
parts of a row and which part reaches the model.

00:15:46.100 --> 00:15:51.064
The second supplied export, U N S W, contains
seventy nine

00:15:51.076 --> 00:15:55.338
thousand, eight hundred eighty one packet rows
and ten original

00:15:55.351 --> 00:15:55.915
labels.

00:15:56.328 --> 00:16:00.076
Its rows follow exactly the same column
structure as the first

00:16:00.089 --> 00:16:04.075
file: fifteen hundred payload byte slots, four
metadata fields,

00:16:04.313 --> 00:16:04.902
and a label.

00:16:05.303 --> 00:16:08.963
That shared structure makes a common packet
preparation route

00:16:08.963 --> 00:16:09.590
possible.

00:16:10.003 --> 00:16:14.002
The examples and original category vocabulary
are different.

00:16:14.453 --> 00:16:18.552
For instance, ordinary traffic is labeled normal
here, while

00:16:18.564 --> 00:16:21.497
other registered names include generic and
exploits.

00:16:21.898 --> 00:16:25.822
A familiar table layout is useful compatibility
evidence, but it

00:16:25.822 --> 00:16:29.319
does not make the underlying traffic or its
supplied annotations

00:16:29.319 --> 00:16:29.883
identical.

00:16:30.334 --> 00:16:34.784
To train one binary classifier, preparation
gives both sources a

00:16:34.797 --> 00:16:36.288
shared target definition.

00:16:36.690 --> 00:16:41.504
C I C&#x27;s benign label and U N S W&#x27;s normal label
become the

00:16:41.516 --> 00:16:42.594
benign category.

00:16:43.008 --> 00:16:45.202
The other registered labels become attack.

00:16:45.616 --> 00:16:49.251
This mapping creates a compatible learning
question; it does not

00:16:49.251 --> 00:16:52.611
assert that all the original attack names mean
the same thing.

00:16:53.025 --> 00:16:56.522
Their source names and counts remain available
for analysis.

00:16:56.924 --> 00:17:01.286
Dataset identity stays outside the neural
forward pass.

00:17:01.700 --> 00:17:05.499
We have also not independently established that
every supplied

00:17:05.599 --> 00:17:08.407
annotation reflects operational maliciousness.

00:17:08.808 --> 00:17:12.857
The model learns the provided labeling task,
with those limits

00:17:12.870 --> 00:17:14.725
carried into its evaluation.

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:18.923
Combining the files means bringing compatible
packet examples

00:17:19.074 --> 00:17:20.189
into the same study.

00:17:20.603 --> 00:17:24.702
Conceptually, we place their example collections
together, while

00:17:24.702 --> 00:17:26.344
preserving the source records.

00:17:26.746 --> 00:17:29.930
We do not match row one in one file with row one
in the

00:17:29.930 --> 00:17:33.552
other, or append one packet&#x27;s bytes to another
packet&#x27;s bytes.

00:17:33.954 --> 00:17:37.138
That would invent a relationship the data never
established.

00:17:37.551 --> 00:17:40.923
The joint training procedure draws selected
groups from both

00:17:40.923 --> 00:17:43.456
sources to teach one classifier.

00:17:43.907 --> 00:17:47.655
Think of combining two sets of practice cards
that ask the same

00:17:47.667 --> 00:17:48.445
kind of question.

00:17:48.858 --> 00:17:52.444
Each card remains a separate example, even
though both sets

00:17:52.444 --> 00:17:53.697
contribute to learning.

00:17:54.167 --> 00:17:57.050
Duplicate checking must cover both files
together.

00:17:57.476 --> 00:18:00.961
The complete preparation identifies four hundred
seventy eight

00:18:00.974 --> 00:18:04.459
thousand, forty four globally distinct payloads.

00:18:04.873 --> 00:18:08.947
Global means the same stored bytes count as one
identity even if

00:18:08.947 --> 00:18:10.627
they appear in different sources.

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:15.666
The code computes a fingerprint called S H A two
fifty six from

00:18:15.817 --> 00:18:17.735
all fifteen hundred canonical bytes.

00:18:18.148 --> 00:18:22.273
This provides a practical identity for grouping
exact payloads,

00:18:22.661 --> 00:18:25.532
and the preparation checks matching payload
content.

00:18:25.933 --> 00:18:30.083
Original row counts and source labels remain
associated with that

00:18:30.083 --> 00:18:30.622
identity.

00:18:31.023 --> 00:18:35.799
A fingerprint describes exact stored content; it
does not reveal

00:18:35.811 --> 00:18:38.306
which physical connection or capture produced
it.

00:18:38.767 --> 00:18:42.177
Ten exact payloads appear in both supplied
files.

00:18:42.579 --> 00:18:46.252
Nine of those shared payloads have conflicting
binary labels.

00:18:46.653 --> 00:18:50.415
That means identical model input can be
associated with benign in

00:18:50.415 --> 00:18:52.672
some rows and attack in others.

00:18:53.085 --> 00:18:55.982
The model is missing the context that might
explain the

00:18:55.982 --> 00:18:59.505
difference, and the labels have not been
independently

00:18:59.505 --> 00:19:00.207
adjudicated.

00:19:00.608 --> 00:19:03.931
We do not fix this by deleting the inconvenient
answer or

00:19:03.943 --> 00:19:05.448
declaring one source correct.

00:19:05.862 --> 00:19:08.169
The original counts remain visible.

00:19:08.570 --> 00:19:11.955
These nine are the conflicting groups among the
shared payloads;

00:19:12.231 --> 00:19:15.616
this is not a claim that the whole combined
dataset contains only

00:19:15.616 --> 00:19:17.484
nine disagreements of this kind.

00:19:17.921 --> 00:19:21.007
Use an illustrative group to see how the
training handles

00:19:21.132 --> 00:19:21.872
disagreement.

00:19:22.274 --> 00:19:26.864
Suppose identical bytes occur in nine rows
labeled benign and one

00:19:26.864 --> 00:19:28.068
row labeled attack.

00:19:28.482 --> 00:19:31.856
The group&#x27;s target is ninety percent benign and
ten percent

00:19:31.869 --> 00:19:32.358
attack.

00:19:32.747 --> 00:19:36.685
Those are proportions of supplied labels, not a
newly verified

00:19:36.685 --> 00:19:38.592
probability about network behavior.

00:19:39.006 --> 00:19:42.167
Each sampled group has equal total weight in the
learning

00:19:42.167 --> 00:19:45.804
calculation, with that weight divided according
to its answers.

00:19:46.218 --> 00:19:49.466
This preserves the contradiction while
preventing a highly

00:19:49.466 --> 00:19:53.919
repeated payload from automatically dominating
the training loss.

00:19:54.321 --> 00:19:58.246
Later, the evidence can distinguish weighting
original rows from

00:19:58.259 --> 00:20:00.780
giving each payload one total vote.

00:20:01.234 --> 00:20:04.469
Each global payload group receives a partition
through a hash

00:20:04.469 --> 00:20:06.852
rule using split seed seventeen.

00:20:07.253 --> 00:20:09.272
Training groups teach the weights.

00:20:09.673 --> 00:20:12.357
Validation groups select the saved checkpoint.

00:20:12.771 --> 00:20:16.006
Test groups measure it after selection has
frozen.

00:20:16.420 --> 00:20:20.747
The target proportions are seventy, fifteen, and
fifteen percent,

00:20:21.135 --> 00:20:24.584
with later caps selecting groups inside those
partitions.

00:20:24.985 --> 00:20:28.961
Keeping identical payloads together prevents
exact copies from

00:20:28.961 --> 00:20:32.660
leaking from practice into the exam, including
across both

00:20:32.660 --> 00:20:33.337
sources.

00:20:33.726 --> 00:20:37.476
The animated symbols represent whole groups that
stay intact.

00:20:37.877 --> 00:20:41.602
This does not remove every near duplicate or
prove capture

00:20:41.602 --> 00:20:42.291
independence.

00:20:42.705 --> 00:20:46.944
These results describe the defined export study,
not guaranteed

00:20:46.956 --> 00:20:48.624
performance on a client&#x27;s network.

00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:59.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Why group both
sources before splitting, and what uncertainty
remains?

00:20:59.138 --> 00:21:03.271
Follow one table row from the supplied CSV into
the classifier.

00:21:03.684 --> 00:21:07.328
Its payload contains fifteen hundred stored
bytes in a fixed

00:21:07.328 --> 00:21:07.717
order.

00:21:08.117 --> 00:21:11.599
These bytes are the observation available to the
model, and

00:21:11.599 --> 00:21:13.691
changing their order can change the pattern.

00:21:14.104 --> 00:21:19.101
The metadata records length, time to live,
protocol, and time

00:21:19.101 --> 00:21:21.806
delta, but stays outside neural input.

00:21:22.219 --> 00:21:25.488
The classifier receives only the transformed
payload.

00:21:25.889 --> 00:21:29.496
During training, a label supplies the answer
used to score its

00:21:29.496 --> 00:21:30.460
prediction afterward.

00:21:30.861 --> 00:21:35.044
Source identity and that answer never enter the
forward

00:21:35.044 --> 00:21:35.770
calculation.

00:21:36.183 --> 00:21:39.916
The diagram separates the branches so you can
point to exactly

00:21:40.128 --> 00:21:41.268
what informs a prediction.

00:21:41.734 --> 00:21:45.567
The adapter converts the validated bytes into
floating point

00:21:45.567 --> 00:21:47.834
numbers, which can represent fractions.

00:21:48.248 --> 00:21:52.507
It divides each byte by one hundred twenty seven
point five and

00:21:52.507 --> 00:21:53.334
subtracts one.

00:21:53.734 --> 00:21:57.430
Zero becomes negative one, and two hundred fifty
five becomes

00:21:57.430 --> 00:21:58.269
positive one.

00:21:58.670 --> 00:22:02.077
This changes the numerical scale while
preserving the byte order.

00:22:02.491 --> 00:22:05.886
A tensor is an array with named dimensions in
our explanation:

00:22:06.337 --> 00:22:09.969
the batch, two dimensions of size one, and
fifteen hundred byte

00:22:09.969 --> 00:22:10.658
positions.

00:22:11.072 --> 00:22:15.030
Stored zeros remain because the export does not
establish which

00:22:15.043 --> 00:22:16.534
zeros represent padding.

00:22:16.935 --> 00:22:20.818
Removing them would silently change the
observation supplied to

00:22:20.818 --> 00:22:21.432
this model.

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:24.443
Start with fifteen hundred bytes from one
packet.

00:22:24.857 --> 00:22:29.317
Grouping and embedding turn each four
neighboring bytes into one

00:22:29.317 --> 00:22:32.812
learned vector, producing three hundred seventy
five vectors.

00:22:33.226 --> 00:22:37.623
The assembled sequence adds three learned
tokens, bringing its

00:22:37.623 --> 00:22:39.903
length to three hundred seventy eight.

00:22:40.317 --> 00:22:43.624
Each position contains two hundred fifty six
numerical

00:22:43.624 --> 00:22:44.339
coordinates.

00:22:44.815 --> 00:22:48.824
Four Mamba blocks transform that sequence using
the original

00:22:48.836 --> 00:22:49.889
encoder machinery.

00:22:50.340 --> 00:22:54.010
Finally, class logits provide two scores after
the summary

00:22:54.010 --> 00:22:56.955
outputs are added and passed through the binary
head.

00:22:57.368 --> 00:23:01.377
These are numerical processing stages, not words
in a language

00:23:01.377 --> 00:23:01.765
model.

00:23:02.179 --> 00:23:06.075
The animation is a schematic of the computation,
not a live

00:23:06.075 --> 00:23:06.802
execution.

00:23:07.279 --> 00:23:11.311
The size stream contains no observations, and
the interval stream

00:23:11.474 --> 00:23:13.703
also contains no observations.

00:23:14.104 --> 00:23:17.860
Interval means a gap between arrivals; these
CSVs do not

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:20.527
establish the flow timing needed for that input.

00:23:20.928 --> 00:23:24.434
Their two learned prefixes remain trainable
model parameters,

00:23:24.822 --> 00:23:27.302
beginning identically for each packet.

00:23:27.715 --> 00:23:31.308
The native encoder scans in a causal order,
meaning earlier

00:23:31.321 --> 00:23:33.813
positions cannot read later positions.

00:23:34.226 --> 00:23:38.070
These prefix outputs therefore cannot summarize
bytes that appear

00:23:38.083 --> 00:23:41.676
afterward, although later byte positions can use
the prefixes.

00:23:42.077 --> 00:23:46.460
Calling all three positions summaries of the
packet would conceal

00:23:46.460 --> 00:23:47.274
this distinction.

00:23:47.674 --> 00:23:50.692
Empty streams are an explicit part of the
adaptation.

00:23:51.167 --> 00:23:53.896
The sequence enters four native Mamba blocks.

00:23:54.297 --> 00:23:58.442
Each block updates a learned numerical state as
it processes the

00:23:58.504 --> 00:23:59.644
ordered representation.

00:24:00.057 --> 00:24:04.026
The fusion step adds the three designated output
vectors, and the

00:24:04.026 --> 00:24:07.682
binary head turns that combined vector into two
logits.

00:24:08.071 --> 00:24:12.203
A head is the final scoring layer; a logit is a
raw class score.

00:24:12.616 --> 00:24:15.446
One score represents benign and the other
attack.

00:24:15.859 --> 00:24:19.653
Across the full packet classifier there are one
million eight

00:24:19.665 --> 00:24:23.109
hundred fifty two thousand four hundred sixteen
parameters, the

00:24:23.109 --> 00:24:25.149
adjustable numbers learned during training.

00:24:25.563 --> 00:24:29.119
These parameters are different from the changing
byte values in

00:24:29.319 --> 00:24:30.797
each incoming observation.

00:24:31.267 --> 00:24:35.675
Pretrained initialization starts with useful
numerical settings

00:24:35.675 --> 00:24:37.366
from the authors&#x27; released checkpoint.

00:24:37.766 --> 00:24:42.149
The adapter copies fifty compatible model
tensors and excludes

00:24:42.225 --> 00:24:45.593
thirty one tensors used for reconstructing
hidden inputs.

00:24:45.994 --> 00:24:48.686
A tensor here is a stored array of parameters.

00:24:49.112 --> 00:24:51.154
The binary head starts fresh.

00:24:51.554 --> 00:24:54.998
Learned position values are explicitly selected
from the original

00:24:54.998 --> 00:24:58.530
table, mapping three hundred seventy eight
positions from four

00:24:58.530 --> 00:24:59.406
hundred forty three.

00:24:59.807 --> 00:25:03.802
Scratch initialization uses the same coordinates
from fresh

00:25:03.802 --> 00:25:04.353
weights.

00:25:04.754 --> 00:25:07.985
This preserves a defined comparison, while the
shorter byte

00:25:07.985 --> 00:25:12.205
sequence and empty numeric streams keep the
packet experiment

00:25:12.255 --> 00:25:14.209
distinct from the paper&#x27;s flow experiment.

00:25:14.667 --> 00:25:17.259
Now connect the pieces in ordinary language.

00:25:17.685 --> 00:25:21.430
One packet supplies ordered stored bytes; those
bytes are

00:25:21.443 --> 00:25:23.923
rescaled and grouped into learned vectors.

00:25:24.373 --> 00:25:27.868
Three learned tokens join the sequence, four
encoder blocks

00:25:27.868 --> 00:25:31.688
process it, and a binary head produces the final
scores.

00:25:32.101 --> 00:25:34.656
The optional metadata has not joined that
journey.

00:25:35.057 --> 00:25:38.864
The label helps training judge a prediction, but
does not enter

00:25:38.877 --> 00:25:40.831
the forward calculation that produces it.

00:25:41.219 --> 00:25:45.102
This model performs numerical traffic
classification without

00:25:45.114 --> 00:25:46.392
calling a chat service.

00:25:46.805 --> 00:25:51.088
Use the coming pause to explain both the
information it receives

00:25:51.351 --> 00:25:54.971
and the information its input contract
deliberately leaves

00:25:54.984 --> 00:25:55.635
outside.

00:25:56.000 --> 00:26:06.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Explain how one
packet becomes two scores, including what stays
outside.

00:26:06.088 --> 00:26:08.948
Training begins with supplied answers, including
their

00:26:08.948 --> 00:26:09.889
disagreements.

00:26:10.291 --> 00:26:15.272
Imagine one identical payload recorded nine
times as benign and

00:26:15.284 --> 00:26:16.351
once as attack.

00:26:16.752 --> 00:26:20.617
The group receives a soft target of ninety
percent benign and ten

00:26:20.617 --> 00:26:21.445
percent attack.

00:26:21.846 --> 00:26:25.560
Soft means the answer preserves proportions
instead of forcing

00:26:25.635 --> 00:26:26.614
one label to win.

00:26:27.003 --> 00:26:30.717
When that group is sampled, its total
contribution to the

00:26:30.717 --> 00:26:34.079
learning loss is one group, divided according to
those

00:26:34.079 --> 00:26:34.857
proportions.

00:26:35.258 --> 00:26:40.001
Repeated copies do not acquire extra total
influence simply by

00:26:40.014 --> 00:26:40.917
being numerous.

00:26:41.354 --> 00:26:44.727
An experimental arm is one defined training
comparison.

00:26:45.141 --> 00:26:49.717
Here the source choice is C I C alone, U N S W
alone,

00:26:50.144 --> 00:26:50.996
or both together.

00:26:51.410 --> 00:26:54.908
Each choice runs with transferred pretrained
weights and with

00:26:54.921 --> 00:26:57.679
fresh scratch weights, giving six arms.

00:26:58.093 --> 00:27:02.180
All use the same packet architecture and one
model seed, zero.

00:27:02.581 --> 00:27:05.666
A seed fixes the recorded random choices for
this run.

00:27:06.079 --> 00:27:10.067
Paired pretrained and scratch arms have
identical fresh heads and

00:27:10.079 --> 00:27:14.442
sampled groups, helping isolate the
initialization comparison.

00:27:14.831 --> 00:27:19.708
One seed still gives only one observed run per
arm; it does not

00:27:19.708 --> 00:27:22.993
describe variation across many independently
repeated training

00:27:23.006 --> 00:27:23.520
runs.

00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:26.760
One update means one cycle through this diagram.

00:27:27.161 --> 00:27:30.222
Sample sixty four training groups, with
replacement.

00:27:30.623 --> 00:27:34.512
The forward calculation produces two scores for
each payload.

00:27:34.938 --> 00:27:37.572
The loss compares those scores with the supplied
label

00:27:37.572 --> 00:27:38.337
proportions.

00:27:38.739 --> 00:27:42.991
Backward computation finds gradients, numbers
that describe how

00:27:42.991 --> 00:27:45.173
weight changes could reduce that loss.

00:27:45.575 --> 00:27:48.936
The optimizer then changes the weights,
completing the update.

00:27:49.350 --> 00:27:52.599
Joint batches contain thirty two groups from
each source.

00:27:52.988 --> 00:27:57.604
Every arm completed one thousand updates, or
sixty four thousand

00:27:57.616 --> 00:27:58.745
group presentations.

00:27:59.134 --> 00:28:01.906
Repeatedly presenting the same group is allowed.

00:28:02.295 --> 00:28:06.296
An update is not an epoch over the complete
uploaded tables, and

00:28:06.309 --> 00:28:07.965
it does not modify the labels.

00:28:08.434 --> 00:28:12.373
The selected training pool contains one hundred
thousand C I C

00:28:12.386 --> 00:28:17.040
groups and twenty seven thousand three hundred
eighty U N S W

00:28:17.040 --> 00:28:17.542
groups.

00:28:17.943 --> 00:28:21.845
Joint batches draw thirty two from each source,
keeping the two

00:28:21.845 --> 00:28:25.057
sources equally represented within each batch.

00:28:25.458 --> 00:28:28.921
Sampling uses replacement, which means a group
can appear again

00:28:29.084 --> 00:28:31.493
while another selected group never appears.

00:28:31.907 --> 00:28:36.461
Across one thousand updates, each model receives
sixty four

00:28:36.473 --> 00:28:38.217
thousand group presentations.

00:28:38.606 --> 00:28:41.793
An epoch would mean a pass through a defined
training dataset.

00:28:42.194 --> 00:28:47.087
These presentations are not full CSV epochs, and
validating all

00:28:47.100 --> 00:28:49.998
original rows does not mean training on all of
them.

00:28:50.467 --> 00:28:54.381
Validation runs every hundred updates, using
groups separate from

00:28:54.381 --> 00:28:54.857
training.

00:28:55.259 --> 00:28:59.373
Its selection score is group weighted macro F
one: a measure that

00:28:59.373 --> 00:29:03.701
combines precision and recall for each class and
averages the two

00:29:03.701 --> 00:29:04.391
classes.

00:29:04.805 --> 00:29:08.781
Precision asks how often a predicted class is
correct; recall

00:29:08.793 --> 00:29:10.989
asks how much of that class was found.

00:29:11.403 --> 00:29:14.451
Joint selection averages the two source scores.

00:29:14.865 --> 00:29:18.603
The earliest checkpoint with the best score wins
any tie.

00:29:19.017 --> 00:29:22.629
All six selections freeze before the final test
predictions.

00:29:23.043 --> 00:29:26.355
This order lets test groups measure the selected
models without

00:29:26.367 --> 00:29:29.867
using their answers to choose which checkpoint
looks most

00:29:29.867 --> 00:29:30.519
attractive.

00:29:30.988 --> 00:29:34.977
A frozen protocol records what experiment will
be run before its

00:29:34.977 --> 00:29:36.119
final results are known.

00:29:36.520 --> 00:29:40.095
It binds the data manifest, original source
identities,

00:29:40.321 --> 00:29:43.921
implementation files, sampling rules, and
training budget.

00:29:44.323 --> 00:29:48.764
Binding means recording hashes that identify
exact file contents.

00:29:49.153 --> 00:29:52.665
A hash can reveal a changed file, although it
cannot prove that

00:29:52.665 --> 00:29:53.782
an annotation is true.

00:29:54.183 --> 00:29:58.172
Checkpoints preserve the relevant protocol and
data identities

00:29:58.298 --> 00:29:59.803
alongside their weights.

00:30:00.205 --> 00:30:04.808
Training receipts record achieved updates,
selection history, and

00:30:04.821 --> 00:30:06.025
optimizer counters.

00:30:06.439 --> 00:30:10.240
Those counters establish that training continued
even when an

00:30:10.290 --> 00:30:12.034
earlier checkpoint was selected.

00:30:12.475 --> 00:30:16.099
Before opening the code, explain three different
uses of data.

00:30:16.488 --> 00:30:19.899
Training groups change model weights through
repeated learning

00:30:19.899 --> 00:30:20.563
updates.

00:30:20.977 --> 00:30:24.877
Validation groups choose a saved checkpoint
according to the rule

00:30:24.990 --> 00:30:26.294
fixed in advance.

00:30:26.695 --> 00:30:29.905
Inference applies selected weights to
observations without

00:30:29.918 --> 00:30:34.394
another learning update; final test inference
adds known answers

00:30:34.407 --> 00:30:35.987
only to calculate measurements.

00:30:36.401 --> 00:30:40.213
The client uses the joint pretrained selection,
so both sources

00:30:40.213 --> 00:30:42.859
contributed supervised training examples.

00:30:43.272 --> 00:30:47.385
The separate split seed, seventeen, assigned
payload groups to

00:30:47.398 --> 00:30:50.859
partitions; it is different from the model seed,
zero.

00:30:51.260 --> 00:30:55.361
Use the pause to identify which stage changes
weights, which

00:30:55.373 --> 00:30:57.643
chooses them, and which measures them.

00:30:58.000 --> 00:31:08.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Explain which data
changes weights, chooses a checkpoint, and
measures it.

00:31:08.113 --> 00:31:11.371
The files divide the workflow into
responsibilities that are

00:31:11.508 --> 00:31:13.350
easier to inspect separately.

00:31:13.764 --> 00:31:17.247
The data module validates the supplied tables
and prepares

00:31:17.260 --> 00:31:18.137
payload groups.

00:31:18.538 --> 00:31:21.683
The model module connects packet bytes to the
pinned native

00:31:21.683 --> 00:31:24.891
encoder and manages compatible checkpoints.

00:31:25.254 --> 00:31:29.489
Shared study utilities calculate measurements
and handle evidence

00:31:29.502 --> 00:31:30.040
records.

00:31:30.441 --> 00:31:34.251
The training program runs the frozen comparisons
and records

00:31:34.251 --> 00:31:35.391
their selected weights.

00:31:35.817 --> 00:31:39.125
The prediction program applies a selected
checkpoint to an

00:31:39.137 --> 00:31:40.390
unlabeled CSV.

00:31:40.842 --> 00:31:44.687
A prepared batch becomes an unsigned byte
tensor, meaning an

00:31:44.700 --> 00:31:48.107
array whose entries hold integers from zero
through two hundred

00:31:48.107 --> 00:31:48.884
fifty five.

00:31:49.272 --> 00:31:52.567
Its dimensions are the batch size and fifteen
hundred bytes.

00:31:52.955 --> 00:31:56.588
The forward call passes this payload tensor into
the adapter and

00:31:56.588 --> 00:31:58.717
returns two logits per observation.

00:31:59.118 --> 00:32:02.726
The adapter handles normalization and constructs
the empty

00:32:02.726 --> 00:32:04.968
numeric streams we already examined.

00:32:05.369 --> 00:32:08.839
The training answers exist in a different tensor
and never enter

00:32:08.839 --> 00:32:09.327
this call.

00:32:09.800 --> 00:32:13.158
The learning loop first clears gradients left
from the previous

00:32:13.158 --> 00:32:13.622
update.

00:32:14.023 --> 00:32:18.572
It obtains logits, then computes cross entropy
against the soft

00:32:18.572 --> 00:32:19.211
targets.

00:32:19.587 --> 00:32:23.960
Cross entropy is the loss measuring how poorly
the scores fit

00:32:24.085 --> 00:32:25.539
those target proportions.

00:32:25.965 --> 00:32:30.062
Backward calculation finds gradients: numerical
sensitivities

00:32:30.175 --> 00:32:33.157
showing how parameter changes affect the loss.

00:32:33.546 --> 00:32:36.202
The optimizer uses them to update the model.

00:32:36.590 --> 00:32:40.074
The complete implementation also rejects invalid
numerical

00:32:40.074 --> 00:32:44.322
values, limits gradient size, and checks that
learning signals

00:32:44.322 --> 00:32:47.855
reach the byte embedding, every encoder block,
and the head.

00:32:48.269 --> 00:32:51.840
Those checks establish an actual learning path;
held out

00:32:51.853 --> 00:32:55.324
measurements still determine whether its
predictions are useful.

00:32:55.788 --> 00:32:59.284
Read the joint pretrained selection from the
frozen checkpoint

00:32:59.297 --> 00:32:59.648
record.

00:33:00.049 --> 00:33:04.059
That entry identifies the saved weights chosen
by validation,

00:33:04.448 --> 00:33:06.027
including their file identity.

00:33:06.428 --> 00:33:09.599
The model loader strictly checks the packet
architecture,

00:33:10.050 --> 00:33:14.449
expected parameter arrays, class mapping, and
provenance, which

00:33:14.449 --> 00:33:15.890
records where the model came from.

00:33:16.304 --> 00:33:19.876
The prediction program then places the loaded
model in evaluation

00:33:19.876 --> 00:33:22.595
mode and applies it without learning updates.

00:33:22.996 --> 00:33:26.794
This is why the released pretraining file cannot
replace the

00:33:26.794 --> 00:33:30.127
selected packet checkpoint: the client needs the
binary

00:33:30.140 --> 00:33:32.872
classifier produced by supervised training.

00:33:33.261 --> 00:33:37.045
Keeping the weights together with their
identifying record makes

00:33:37.058 --> 00:33:38.750
a later prediction traceable.

00:33:39.234 --> 00:33:42.716
The prediction CSV accepts fifteen hundred
payload columns,

00:33:43.129 --> 00:33:45.747
optionally followed by the four metadata
columns.

00:33:46.123 --> 00:33:50.319
A label column is rejected, so create a separate
unlabeled copy.

00:33:50.745 --> 00:33:55.092
Header order, row width, and payload byte
validity are checked.

00:33:55.492 --> 00:33:59.113
A cap of one hundred twenty eight limits
predicted rows while the

00:33:59.125 --> 00:34:02.019
program still scans the entire file.

00:34:02.407 --> 00:34:06.077
Predictions may be computed during that scan;
successful

00:34:06.077 --> 00:34:08.682
completion requires validation to finish.

00:34:09.058 --> 00:34:13.918
A malformed later row prevents a successful
completed result even

00:34:13.918 --> 00:34:15.898
if earlier predictions were calculated.

00:34:16.311 --> 00:34:20.394
The receipt reports validated and predicted
counts separately, so

00:34:20.394 --> 00:34:23.513
a capped run cannot claim prediction coverage it
lacks.

00:34:23.967 --> 00:34:27.636
Each prediction record includes the row
identity, payload hash,

00:34:27.887 --> 00:34:31.093
two logits, class name, and probabilities.

00:34:31.481 --> 00:34:35.263
The larger logit chooses the class, with an
exact tie assigned to

00:34:35.263 --> 00:34:35.689
benign.

00:34:36.089 --> 00:34:40.134
Softmax converts the logits into nonnegative
numbers that sum to

00:34:40.134 --> 00:34:43.666
one; it does not supply independent evidence
about the customer&#x27;s

00:34:43.666 --> 00:34:44.179
network.

00:34:44.580 --> 00:34:48.487
These scores are uncalibrated, meaning their
numerical size has

00:34:48.487 --> 00:34:52.507
not been established as a reliable frequency of
real malicious

00:34:52.507 --> 00:34:53.083
events.

00:34:53.496 --> 00:34:57.404
The separate receipt ties outputs to input,
checkpoint, and code

00:34:57.404 --> 00:34:58.105
identities.

00:34:58.567 --> 00:35:02.236
The native packet gate checks whether the actual
model operates

00:35:02.362 --> 00:35:03.564
in the prepared environment.

00:35:03.977 --> 00:35:08.223
Its recorded run on G B ten passed seventeen
tests with none

00:35:08.235 --> 00:35:11.479
skipped, using two stored payloads from each
source.

00:35:11.892 --> 00:35:15.586
It exercises compatible weight transfer, tensor
shapes,

00:35:15.787 --> 00:35:19.807
gradients, an optimizer update, and strict
checkpoint loading

00:35:19.819 --> 00:35:20.446
after saving.

00:35:20.846 --> 00:35:24.654
Synthetic targets supply a controlled learning
signal for these

00:35:24.654 --> 00:35:25.255
checks.

00:35:25.655 --> 00:35:29.751
They do not measure benchmark accuracy or repeat
the six arm

00:35:29.863 --> 00:35:30.627
training study.

00:35:31.000 --> 00:35:41.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Explain what a
successful capped prediction receipt proves
about the input.

00:35:41.101 --> 00:35:43.669
Start with the delivered joint pretrained model.

00:35:44.071 --> 00:35:47.860
It reached ninety seven point five six percent
group balanced

00:35:47.873 --> 00:35:52.795
accuracy on C I C and ninety four point two nine
percent on U

00:35:52.920 --> 00:35:53.588
N S W.

00:35:54.003 --> 00:35:57.704
The test sets contain twenty thousand and five
thousand nine

00:35:57.704 --> 00:36:01.807
hundred thirty distinct payload groups,
respectively, totaling

00:36:01.807 --> 00:36:04.690
twenty five thousand nine hundred thirty
predictions.

00:36:05.093 --> 00:36:08.000
These groups came from the partitions reserved
earlier.

00:36:08.403 --> 00:36:10.606
The selected weights were already frozen.

00:36:11.009 --> 00:36:15.226
This ties the headline to a defined model, unit,
and evaluation

00:36:15.238 --> 00:36:15.641
set.

00:36:16.044 --> 00:36:19.606
Carry those conditions with the percentages
whenever you explain

00:36:19.820 --> 00:36:22.048
what the study established to another person.

00:36:22.501 --> 00:36:26.554
Balanced accuracy averages benign recall and
attack recall,

00:36:26.844 --> 00:36:29.160
giving the two classes equal importance.

00:36:29.562 --> 00:36:33.012
Recall means the fraction of a supplied class
correctly

00:36:33.024 --> 00:36:33.767
recognized.

00:36:34.182 --> 00:36:38.286
Group weighting gives each distinct payload one
total vote,

00:36:38.588 --> 00:36:40.652
divided across conflicting labels.

00:36:41.055 --> 00:36:44.869
Row weighting gives every original row a vote,
so repeated

00:36:44.882 --> 00:36:46.594
payloads can change the outcome.

00:36:47.009 --> 00:36:50.320
With row weighting, the joint scores become
ninety six point

00:36:50.320 --> 00:36:54.877
three one percent on C I C and eighty nine point
two four on

00:36:54.877 --> 00:36:55.745
U N S W.

00:36:56.148 --> 00:36:59.811
The model has not changed between these
calculations; the weights

00:36:59.874 --> 00:37:02.744
assigned to the evaluation observations have
changed.

00:37:03.188 --> 00:37:07.240
Read each row as one trained model and each
column as a test

00:37:07.240 --> 00:37:07.743
source.

00:37:08.158 --> 00:37:12.487
The C I C pretrained model performs strongly on
its own source

00:37:12.814 --> 00:37:16.665
but reaches only fifty three point seven three
percent on U N S

00:37:16.677 --> 00:37:17.030
W.

00:37:17.533 --> 00:37:21.094
Transfer means applying learned weights to
another source without

00:37:21.107 --> 00:37:23.183
supervised fitting on that source.

00:37:23.585 --> 00:37:27.134
Joint training changes this comparison: against
the corresponding

00:37:27.147 --> 00:37:30.594
pretrained source models, it gains one point
zero seven

00:37:30.594 --> 00:37:35.451
percentage points on U N S W and loses one point
one one on

00:37:35.451 --> 00:37:36.295
C I C.

00:37:36.710 --> 00:37:40.283
These are observed tradeoffs in this run, not a
universal

00:37:40.283 --> 00:37:40.825
benefit.

00:37:41.301 --> 00:37:45.456
Scratch models expose an important weak result
in the completed

00:37:45.469 --> 00:37:45.884
study.

00:37:46.287 --> 00:37:50.594
The C I C only and U N S W only selections both
score

00:37:50.606 --> 00:37:54.132
fifty percent balanced accuracy on both test
sources.

00:37:54.535 --> 00:37:58.929
Their decisions stay in a single class: one
class receives full

00:37:58.929 --> 00:38:03.311
recall and the other receives none, averaging to
fifty percent.

00:38:03.727 --> 00:38:07.202
Both checkpoints were selected at update one
hundred, although

00:38:07.202 --> 00:38:09.796
their training continued to one thousand.

00:38:10.212 --> 00:38:14.279
Joint scratch reaches fifty five point five
seven percent on C I

00:38:14.279 --> 00:38:17.339
C and fifty percent on U N S W.

00:38:17.905 --> 00:38:20.650
Completed training therefore includes models
that failed to

00:38:20.650 --> 00:38:21.922
discriminate usefully.

00:38:22.401 --> 00:38:25.069
Nine controls help interpret the neural results.

00:38:25.484 --> 00:38:29.713
Each source choice uses three methods: a
majority classifier,

00:38:29.927 --> 00:38:33.060
which always predicts the class with more
weighted training group

00:38:33.073 --> 00:38:37.050
target mass; a histogram model, which counts
byte frequencies;

00:38:37.490 --> 00:38:40.901
and a metadata model, which uses recorded
properties.

00:38:41.303 --> 00:38:44.966
Metadata stays outside the neural model&#x27;s
inputs.

00:38:45.368 --> 00:38:49.962
The U N S W only metadata control reaches
ninety-nine point

00:38:49.962 --> 00:38:52.617
thirty-four percent group balanced accuracy.

00:38:53.007 --> 00:38:56.267
It exceeds every native model tested on that
source.

00:38:56.682 --> 00:39:00.445
These recorded properties strongly distinguish
this source&#x27;s

00:39:00.445 --> 00:39:01.037
labels.

00:39:01.439 --> 00:39:05.051
The methods use different inputs, and their
results do not

00:39:05.051 --> 00:39:08.122
establish that neural models are uniformly
superior.

00:39:08.601 --> 00:39:12.792
The original full inference check reproduced
every class across

00:39:12.792 --> 00:39:16.455
twenty five thousand nine hundred thirty
predictions, while two

00:39:16.455 --> 00:39:18.897
rows failed the strict logit comparison.

00:39:19.300 --> 00:39:24.423
A separate fresh client run validated twenty
thousand C I C rows

00:39:24.662 --> 00:39:26.349
and predicted one hundred twenty eight.

00:39:26.739 --> 00:39:30.805
All those classes agreed, but seventy six rows
failed the strict

00:39:30.818 --> 00:39:31.951
numerical comparison.

00:39:32.353 --> 00:39:36.293
Both checks used relative tolerance one ten
thousandth and

00:39:36.394 --> 00:39:38.232
absolute tolerance one millionth.

00:39:38.635 --> 00:39:41.807
A tolerance defines the allowed numerical
difference.

00:39:42.209 --> 00:39:45.948
The fresh maximum difference is preserved
exactly on screen.

00:39:46.338 --> 00:39:50.089
Agreement on a class does not make the
underlying scores

00:39:50.215 --> 00:39:51.474
numerically identical.

00:39:51.921 --> 00:39:55.183
Report these results with the conditions that
make them

00:39:55.196 --> 00:39:56.014
interpretable.

00:39:56.430 --> 00:40:00.384
The study used one model seed, capped training
pools, and a fixed

00:40:00.384 --> 00:40:01.089
update budget.

00:40:01.505 --> 00:40:04.678
The earlier data seen by the released pretrained
weights is

00:40:04.816 --> 00:40:05.371
unknown.

00:40:05.774 --> 00:40:09.602
Exact payload grouping also cannot prove
independence between

00:40:09.614 --> 00:40:10.622
network captures.

00:40:11.037 --> 00:40:15.193
The selected C I C test has no PortScan rows and
only four

00:40:15.206 --> 00:40:18.958
distributed denial of service rows, so broad
subtype claims would

00:40:18.958 --> 00:40:19.953
exceed its support.

00:40:20.406 --> 00:40:24.889
Finally, the paper&#x27;s ninety seven point five
zero percent flow

00:40:25.053 --> 00:40:28.315
accuracy measures a different task and metric.

00:40:28.730 --> 00:40:32.621
These packet results establish neither its
reproduction nor an

00:40:32.621 --> 00:40:33.629
improvement over it.

00:40:34.000 --> 00:40:44.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] State one measured
success, one weak result, and two remaining
limits.

00:40:44.089 --> 00:40:46.805
Now demonstrate the system to someone else.

00:40:47.211 --> 00:40:50.523
Open the packet viewer from the project home
page, or generate

00:40:50.536 --> 00:40:52.756
the same page from the client repository.

00:40:53.162 --> 00:40:56.627
The viewer displays predictions already produced
by the selected

00:40:56.627 --> 00:40:57.299
joint model.

00:40:57.706 --> 00:41:00.548
It does not train a model or inspect your
current network.

00:41:00.967 --> 00:41:03.277
Say that boundary before clicking anything.

00:41:03.683 --> 00:41:07.084
This makes the demonstration useful: you can
show real model

00:41:07.084 --> 00:41:10.967
decisions and their limitations without
pretending a browser is

00:41:10.967 --> 00:41:12.744
an operational security system.

00:41:13.162 --> 00:41:16.931
The next steps follow the same saved packet
evidence used in the

00:41:16.944 --> 00:41:17.858
results chapter.

00:41:18.335 --> 00:41:22.574
The source selector switches between the C I C
and U N S W

00:41:22.574 --> 00:41:23.361
test groups.

00:41:23.768 --> 00:41:28.045
The C I C view contains twenty thousand distinct
payload groups;

00:41:28.312 --> 00:41:32.247
the U N S W view contains five thousand nine
hundred thirty.

00:41:32.653 --> 00:41:37.007
These are selected test groups, not the original
total CSV row

00:41:37.007 --> 00:41:37.616
counts.

00:41:38.048 --> 00:41:41.462
The headline cards always describe the complete
selected source

00:41:41.462 --> 00:41:42.084
test set.

00:41:42.503 --> 00:41:46.235
Table filters only choose which saved rows
appear on screen.

00:41:46.641 --> 00:41:50.081
Explain which source is selected before reading
any metric,

00:41:50.373 --> 00:41:53.407
because the model performs differently on the
two sources.

00:41:53.855 --> 00:41:56.013
Open one prediction&#x27;s detail panel.

00:41:56.431 --> 00:42:00.289
The long hash identifies the exact payload group
without

00:42:00.302 --> 00:42:01.926
displaying the original bytes.

00:42:02.332 --> 00:42:06.316
The model produced two logits, which are
numerical scores before

00:42:06.316 --> 00:42:08.194
conversion into probabilities.

00:42:08.600 --> 00:42:11.544
The larger logit chooses benign or attack.

00:42:11.950 --> 00:42:15.693
The viewer also shows an uncalibrated
probability for that

00:42:15.693 --> 00:42:16.303
choice.

00:42:16.683 --> 00:42:21.264
A large value means the model favors one class
under its learned

00:42:21.264 --> 00:42:21.987
scoring rule.

00:42:22.406 --> 00:42:26.631
It does not prove that the packet is dangerous,
and it is not a

00:42:26.631 --> 00:42:30.413
measured probability that an analyst will
confirm an attack on a

00:42:30.426 --> 00:42:31.339
customer network.

00:42:31.801 --> 00:42:35.061
The same payload can appear in multiple rows
with different

00:42:35.061 --> 00:42:36.000
supplied labels.

00:42:36.418 --> 00:42:40.312
The viewer therefore keeps the benign and attack
row counts for

00:42:40.426 --> 00:42:40.959
each group.

00:42:41.365 --> 00:42:44.599
It does not silently choose a convenient single
label.

00:42:45.043 --> 00:42:49.824
For example, a hypothetical group with nine
benign labels and one

00:42:49.824 --> 00:42:52.995
attack label still contains both kinds of
evidence.

00:42:53.388 --> 00:42:56.762
A benign prediction disagrees with the one
attack-labeled row.

00:42:57.168 --> 00:43:00.897
That example explains the interface; it is not
an additional

00:43:00.897 --> 00:43:01.696
measured result.

00:43:02.102 --> 00:43:05.843
The original labels have not been independently
adjudicated, so

00:43:05.856 --> 00:43:10.447
disagreements can reveal model errors, label
problems, or both.

00:43:10.922 --> 00:43:14.588
Select the disagreement filter and inspect a
result.

00:43:15.019 --> 00:43:18.520
The point is to show how a model can be wrong,
including when its

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:19.585
own score is high.

00:43:19.966 --> 00:43:23.479
Read the predicted class and the original label
counts together.

00:43:23.885 --> 00:43:27.170
If you choose the conflicting-label filter,
explain why

00:43:27.170 --> 00:43:32.028
disagreement is unavoidable for some rows
sharing identical model

00:43:32.028 --> 00:43:32.713
inputs.

00:43:33.106 --> 00:43:37.152
Do not call every displayed disagreement a
proven malicious

00:43:37.152 --> 00:43:38.839
packet that was missed.

00:43:39.258 --> 00:43:43.253
This is a labeled research dataset with a
specific evaluation

00:43:43.266 --> 00:43:43.557
rule.

00:43:43.976 --> 00:43:47.756
A credible presentation explains that rule and
shows an actual

00:43:47.756 --> 00:43:50.609
limitation alongside the positive results.

00:43:51.106 --> 00:43:54.762
Fresh inference happens in the Python packet
predictor using the

00:43:54.762 --> 00:43:58.202
checked GPU environment and a trained joint
checkpoint.

00:43:58.608 --> 00:44:02.493
It accepts a correctly formatted unlabeled CSV
and writes

00:44:02.493 --> 00:44:04.409
predictions plus a receipt.

00:44:04.828 --> 00:44:09.208
The label column must be removed in a separate
input copy because

00:44:09.220 --> 00:44:11.696
inference must not receive the answers.

00:44:12.115 --> 00:44:16.100
The new receipt distinguishes validated rows
from predicted rows

00:44:16.316 --> 00:44:18.055
when a prediction cap is used.

00:44:18.449 --> 00:44:21.546
Opening the viewer and running the predictor
demonstrate

00:44:21.597 --> 00:44:23.133
different parts of this workflow.

00:44:23.539 --> 00:44:27.271
The viewer explains retained results; the
predictor performs a

00:44:27.271 --> 00:44:28.756
new model computation.

00:44:29.209 --> 00:44:32.165
An operational intrusion detection system would
connect

00:44:32.254 --> 00:44:34.994
additional components around this classifier.

00:44:35.413 --> 00:44:38.939
It would capture traffic, extract bytes with the
same meaning as

00:44:38.952 --> 00:44:43.138
the training inputs, queue examples, run
inference, apply a

00:44:43.138 --> 00:44:46.601
reviewed alert policy, and store events for
investigation.

00:44:47.007 --> 00:44:50.813
Monitoring would need to report dropped packets
and processing

00:44:50.813 --> 00:44:51.472
failures.

00:44:51.840 --> 00:44:55.189
A blocking system would need further response
and recovery

00:44:55.189 --> 00:44:55.887
decisions.

00:44:56.280 --> 00:45:00.124
None of those components appears merely because
a prediction says

00:45:00.124 --> 00:45:00.695
attack.

00:45:01.113 --> 00:45:05.033
The working demonstration here is a recorded
packet viewer plus

00:45:05.261 --> 00:45:07.215
an actual CSV inference program.

00:45:07.646 --> 00:45:11.249
The missing operational components remain
explicit engineering

00:45:11.249 --> 00:45:11.642
work.

00:45:12.000 --> 00:45:22.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Explain what the
browser demonstrates and what requires the GPU
predictor.

00:45:22.138 --> 00:45:25.614
For a person who needs to run the delivered
project, start from

00:45:25.614 --> 00:45:26.907
the client repository.

00:45:27.321 --> 00:45:31.249
Clone it, enter its root directory, and read the
setup document

00:45:31.249 --> 00:45:31.576
there.

00:45:31.977 --> 00:45:35.579
The source repository remains useful for
development and the

00:45:35.592 --> 00:45:38.955
longer explanations, but the client contains the
selected

00:45:38.968 --> 00:45:41.252
execution files and their tests.

00:45:41.654 --> 00:45:44.716
Use a new output directory for each experiment.

00:45:45.117 --> 00:45:49.021
That preserves the previous run, its errors, and
its identity

00:45:49.021 --> 00:45:49.648
records.

00:45:50.100 --> 00:45:53.274
The first commands need Git and a supported
Python interpreter,

00:45:53.675 --> 00:45:56.686
without a GPU or the private CSV files.

00:45:57.087 --> 00:46:01.904
Run the tests, verify the delivered inventory
and evidence, and

00:46:01.916 --> 00:46:03.497
generate the packet viewer.

00:46:03.886 --> 00:46:07.323
On the recorded local Linux check, the client
discovered one

00:46:07.323 --> 00:46:08.865
hundred eighty-five tests.

00:46:09.254 --> 00:46:13.005
One hundred forty-nine passed and thirty-six
optional tests

00:46:13.005 --> 00:46:13.431
skipped.

00:46:13.833 --> 00:46:16.830
A skip is not a successful native GPU test.

00:46:17.257 --> 00:46:20.154
Windows had one additional skip in its hosted
jobs.

00:46:20.556 --> 00:46:23.303
These counts belong to the recorded delivery
revision.

00:46:23.692 --> 00:46:27.204
Later software revisions can change the suite,
so retain the run

00:46:27.204 --> 00:46:27.731
output.

00:46:28.188 --> 00:46:31.989
Actual neural execution needs more than the
portable test suite.

00:46:32.390 --> 00:46:36.655
The measured environment used Linux on A R M
sixty-four, an

00:46:36.655 --> 00:46:41.409
NVIDIA G B ten, Python three point twelve, and
Torch two point

00:46:41.409 --> 00:46:43.165
nine point one with CUDA thirteen.

00:46:43.567 --> 00:46:47.054
The compiler toolkit and custom operators also
matter.

00:46:47.443 --> 00:46:51.068
A driver displaying a CUDA version does not mean
the compiler is

00:46:51.080 --> 00:46:51.657
installed.

00:46:52.134 --> 00:46:55.621
Follow the exact package and environment
commands in the setup

00:46:55.634 --> 00:46:56.198
document.

00:46:56.612 --> 00:47:00.488
Other physical GPUs need their own build,
numerical and

00:47:00.488 --> 00:47:01.743
packet-model checks.

00:47:02.144 --> 00:47:06.459
Hosted Windows and macOS checks do not prove
native neural

00:47:06.459 --> 00:47:07.412
execution there.

00:47:07.867 --> 00:47:11.415
The setup obtains the pinned authors&#x27; source and
checks its

00:47:11.428 --> 00:47:11.967
identity.

00:47:12.368 --> 00:47:16.030
The default asset fetch now acquires only the
registered

00:47:16.042 --> 00:47:20.167
pretrained initialization, with its expected
size and hash.

00:47:20.556 --> 00:47:22.913
It does not download a flow dataset.

00:47:23.315 --> 00:47:27.151
It also does not supply your two private CSVs or
a finished

00:47:27.164 --> 00:47:28.694
binary packet classifier.

00:47:29.095 --> 00:47:33.383
You provide the CSVs locally and training
produces the selected

00:47:33.396 --> 00:47:34.361
packet checkpoint.

00:47:34.762 --> 00:47:38.373
This explains why cloning the repository is
enough to inspect

00:47:38.386 --> 00:47:42.636
evidence but not enough to run a trained
application immediately.

00:47:43.050 --> 00:47:47.088
The asset notice records attribution and
unresolved inherited

00:47:47.100 --> 00:47:47.690
terms.

00:47:48.167 --> 00:47:51.805
Run packet preparation with both input paths and
a fresh

00:47:51.805 --> 00:47:52.483
destination.

00:47:52.897 --> 00:47:57.074
It validates every row and byte, creates the
global groups and

00:47:57.074 --> 00:48:00.110
partitions, and saves arrays with a manifest.

00:48:00.549 --> 00:48:03.999
For an exact repeat of the uploaded-file study,
check the

00:48:04.012 --> 00:48:06.759
registered source identities in that manifest.

00:48:07.173 --> 00:48:10.473
The packet runtime gate then uses prepared bytes
from both

00:48:10.485 --> 00:48:15.340
sources to exercise transfer, input shapes,
gradients, an

00:48:15.353 --> 00:48:17.573
optimizer update and checkpoint reload.

00:48:17.987 --> 00:48:22.002
All seventeen cases passed on the recorded G B
ten checks.

00:48:22.403 --> 00:48:25.715
The loss targets in that gate are synthetic, so
its pass is

00:48:25.728 --> 00:48:28.588
functional evidence, not benchmark accuracy.

00:48:29.067 --> 00:48:32.791
The setup gives the full commands to freeze a
protocol, fit the

00:48:32.791 --> 00:48:36.903
controls, train the six comparison arms and
evaluate the frozen

00:48:36.915 --> 00:48:37.480
results.

00:48:37.906 --> 00:48:42.093
Protocol freezing binds the data, implementation
and declared

00:48:42.106 --> 00:48:42.532
budget.

00:48:42.933 --> 00:48:46.456
Read the completed receipts rather than assuming
a requested

00:48:46.456 --> 00:48:47.835
number of updates finished.

00:48:48.224 --> 00:48:52.324
The selected joint pretrained checkpoint is
listed in the frozen

00:48:52.336 --> 00:48:53.314
checkpoint file.

00:48:53.728 --> 00:48:56.072
Use that path for the client&#x27;s prediction
command.

00:48:56.461 --> 00:49:00.084
It belongs to this same packet workflow; the
other arms are

00:49:00.084 --> 00:49:02.617
comparisons that help explain the chosen model.

00:49:03.031 --> 00:49:06.629
Preserve any failure report while investigating
a new run.

00:49:07.100 --> 00:49:11.089
If a check fails, identify whether the failure
concerns input

00:49:11.101 --> 00:49:15.529
format, source identity, a missing operator,
incomplete

00:49:15.592 --> 00:49:17.900
execution, or numerical agreement.

00:49:18.313 --> 00:49:20.584
Those are different problems with different
fixes.

00:49:20.985 --> 00:49:24.622
Keep the failed report and use a new output path
for a corrected

00:49:24.622 --> 00:49:25.287
execution.

00:49:25.676 --> 00:49:29.288
Do not loosen a scientific tolerance just to
make a presentation

00:49:29.288 --> 00:49:29.690
green.

00:49:30.104 --> 00:49:34.230
The current record deliberately retains
score-comparison failures

00:49:34.456 --> 00:49:36.726
even when predicted classes match.

00:49:37.140 --> 00:49:40.915
That makes the project more useful to the next
engineer, who can

00:49:40.928 --> 00:49:45.029
see what was tested, what succeeded, and which
remaining issue

00:49:45.042 --> 00:49:46.635
still requires investigation.

00:49:47.000 --> 00:49:57.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Name the evidence
needed before claiming a new machine runs the
native model.

00:49:57.088 --> 00:50:00.943
There are two repositories because their
audiences need different

00:50:00.943 --> 00:50:01.957
amounts of material.

00:50:02.370 --> 00:50:05.825
The reproduction repository is the development
and research

00:50:05.825 --> 00:50:06.363
source.

00:50:06.776 --> 00:50:11.170
It includes the full explanations, historical
work, presentation

00:50:11.170 --> 00:50:13.198
builders and teaching materials.

00:50:13.598 --> 00:50:17.641
The client repository is a concise executable
delivery with the

00:50:17.654 --> 00:50:21.810
packet code, selected dependencies, tests and
evidence.

00:50:22.198 --> 00:50:24.576
They do not offer competing model workflows.

00:50:24.977 --> 00:50:29.696
Both lead to the same joint packet adaptation
using the two CSVs.

00:50:30.134 --> 00:50:33.138
Tell a presenter to begin with the reproduction
guide, and tell

00:50:33.138 --> 00:50:36.668
the engineer receiving the executable handoff to
begin with the

00:50:36.681 --> 00:50:37.469
client setup.

00:50:37.933 --> 00:50:42.691
The top-level packet data module validates
inputs, maps labels,

00:50:43.079 --> 00:50:46.885
groups identical payloads and constructs
prepared artifacts.

00:50:47.311 --> 00:50:50.415
The packet model module connects the supplied
bytes to the

00:50:50.415 --> 00:50:54.321
original encoder, handles compatible pretrained
transfer and

00:50:54.321 --> 00:50:56.287
defines the binary classifier.

00:50:56.700 --> 00:51:00.744
The packet study module supplies shared
experiment and evidence

00:51:00.756 --> 00:51:01.382
functions.

00:51:01.783 --> 00:51:05.626
Keeping those responsibilities distinct makes
the project easier

00:51:05.639 --> 00:51:06.277
to check.

00:51:06.678 --> 00:51:11.235
If an input row is rejected, investigate
preparation or inference

00:51:11.248 --> 00:51:11.711
parsing.

00:51:12.124 --> 00:51:16.180
If checkpoint shapes disagree, investigate the
model contract.

00:51:16.646 --> 00:51:20.137
The tools directory contains the programs for
source and asset

00:51:20.137 --> 00:51:24.591
acquisition, native builds, runtime checks,
protocol freezing,

00:51:24.829 --> 00:51:29.008
controls, training, prediction, evidence review
and demo

00:51:29.008 --> 00:51:29.446
rendering.

00:51:29.834 --> 00:51:33.437
Configurations record settings and asset
identities.

00:51:33.838 --> 00:51:36.628
Requirements record the measured dependency set.

00:51:37.053 --> 00:51:41.145
The shared reproduction helper remains an
internal dependency for

00:51:41.207 --> 00:51:45.124
upstream identity and build operations, even in
the client.

00:51:45.524 --> 00:51:49.178
Its historical orchestration does not create
another supported

00:51:49.178 --> 00:51:50.166
client workflow.

00:51:50.554 --> 00:51:54.020
Use the documented packet setup rather than
exploring old

00:51:54.032 --> 00:51:56.823
commands as if they were additional product
choices.

00:51:57.210 --> 00:52:01.665
Each tool should have an identifiable input,
output and checkable

00:52:01.665 --> 00:52:02.691
completion condition.

00:52:03.167 --> 00:52:06.259
The public evidence directories contain saved
predictions,

00:52:06.534 --> 00:52:10.653
metrics, protocols, receipts and hashes that
support the

00:52:10.653 --> 00:52:11.617
published claims.

00:52:12.005 --> 00:52:16.174
Local run directories contain machine-specific
execution outputs

00:52:16.562 --> 00:52:18.440
and separately acquired assets.

00:52:18.828 --> 00:52:22.884
Raw CSVs, the authors&#x27; checkout and trained
weights are not

00:52:22.934 --> 00:52:25.012
automatically part of a public source clone.

00:52:25.413 --> 00:52:29.607
In the full reproduction repository, older flow
experiments and

00:52:29.607 --> 00:52:32.736
calibration records remain historical research.

00:52:33.137 --> 00:52:36.166
They help explain earlier work but are not the
present client

00:52:36.166 --> 00:52:36.592
setup.

00:52:37.005 --> 00:52:41.199
The file walkthrough explains individual paths
and downloads so

00:52:41.199 --> 00:52:44.366
you can tell an executable component from a
result, a source

00:52:44.366 --> 00:52:46.131
document or a generated artifact.

00:52:46.600 --> 00:52:50.228
At the recorded packet-only delivery, the client
contained one

00:52:50.240 --> 00:52:53.468
hundred twenty-four files, including its
manifest.

00:52:53.856 --> 00:52:56.608
Seventy-five were packet-study evidence files.

00:52:57.008 --> 00:53:00.599
The manifest records the selected source
revision, the origin of

00:53:00.724 --> 00:53:04.126
each delivered file, its size and its hash.

00:53:04.527 --> 00:53:08.280
A hash answers whether the bytes match a
recorded identity.

00:53:08.680 --> 00:53:11.157
It does not prove that the scientific claim is
true.

00:53:11.557 --> 00:53:15.323
The evidence reviewer adds arithmetic and
consistency checks.

00:53:15.711 --> 00:53:19.126
Together these mechanisms let the recipient
establish which

00:53:19.138 --> 00:53:22.929
software and results they received, while still
reading the

00:53:22.941 --> 00:53:24.930
limits of the actual experiment.

00:53:25.367 --> 00:53:28.959
Development changes belong in the reproduction
repository,

00:53:29.335 --> 00:53:32.627
including the templates used to generate client
instructions.

00:53:33.040 --> 00:53:36.971
After successful source checks, the publication
workflow selects

00:53:36.983 --> 00:53:40.738
the tested source revision and builds an
explicit client export.

00:53:41.126 --> 00:53:45.370
It rejects unexpected client edits and checks
the staged contents

00:53:45.382 --> 00:53:46.471
before publication.

00:53:46.884 --> 00:53:50.514
A separate publishing step updates the client,
whose own platform

00:53:50.514 --> 00:53:51.503
checks then run.

00:53:51.904 --> 00:53:55.872
The packet-only update actually exercised
managed removal of

00:53:55.960 --> 00:53:57.136
older flow files.

00:53:57.549 --> 00:54:00.503
If you manually change the client and it
diverges,

00:54:00.741 --> 00:54:04.784
synchronization stops for review instead of
silently overwriting

00:54:04.784 --> 00:54:05.460
the difference.

00:54:05.861 --> 00:54:07.801
This protects the handoff&#x27;s identity.

00:54:08.267 --> 00:54:13.023
This video, its captions, transcript, slide deck
and handbook

00:54:13.110 --> 00:54:14.963
come from one reviewed lesson source.

00:54:15.363 --> 00:54:18.630
Their records bind the rendered assets to that
source.

00:54:19.030 --> 00:54:22.260
The handbook includes the repository file guide
for details that

00:54:22.260 --> 00:54:24.074
would be tedious to read aloud.

00:54:24.487 --> 00:54:28.054
The shorter packet presentation remains a
concise summary of the

00:54:28.067 --> 00:54:28.880
same study.

00:54:29.281 --> 00:54:32.798
Teaching media belongs in the reproduction
repository and is

00:54:32.798 --> 00:54:34.500
excluded from the client export.

00:54:34.888 --> 00:54:38.443
An explanation-only source edit may therefore
leave the client&#x27;s

00:54:38.618 --> 00:54:40.445
source marker unchanged.

00:54:40.846 --> 00:54:44.525
A selected code or template change follows the
verified update

00:54:44.538 --> 00:54:46.616
process described in the previous scene.

00:54:47.000 --> 00:54:57.000
[Practice pause: 10 seconds] Explain which repo
you would give a presenter and which you would
give an engineer.

00:54:57.100 --> 00:54:58.764
Now consider a future deployment.

00:54:59.177 --> 00:55:03.068
Draw the entire path from observed network
traffic to a usable

00:55:03.081 --> 00:55:04.057
security event.

00:55:04.444 --> 00:55:07.497
Capturing bytes, identifying the correct
representation,

00:55:07.760 --> 00:55:11.676
buffering work, transferring data, running the
classifier and

00:55:11.676 --> 00:55:14.679
recording an alert all take time and can fail.

00:55:15.054 --> 00:55:18.157
A model-only timing would not include those
stages.

00:55:18.570 --> 00:55:22.274
This packet handoff does not establish
operational throughput,

00:55:22.786 --> 00:55:25.151
end-to-end latency or an alert guarantee.

00:55:25.577 --> 00:55:29.192
The first hardware task is to define that
complete path and its

00:55:29.192 --> 00:55:29.968
requirements.

00:55:30.381 --> 00:55:34.372
Only then can an engineering team decide which
stages should run

00:55:34.372 --> 00:55:35.336
on which device.

00:55:35.763 --> 00:55:39.956
A network interface card moves network data into
and out of a

00:55:39.968 --> 00:55:40.444
system.

00:55:40.832 --> 00:55:44.912
A Smart N I C adds programmable processing near
that network

00:55:44.912 --> 00:55:45.401
boundary.

00:55:45.814 --> 00:55:49.944
It could contribute capture, filtering,
buffering or compatible

00:55:49.944 --> 00:55:53.199
preprocessing, depending on the specific
hardware and runtime.

00:55:53.599 --> 00:55:56.916
Those are proposed responsibilities, not
capabilities

00:55:56.991 --> 00:55:58.769
demonstrated by this repository.

00:55:59.157 --> 00:56:02.711
In particular, putting a model file on a network
card does not

00:56:02.711 --> 00:56:06.116
establish correct packet extraction or reliable
inference.

00:56:06.529 --> 00:56:10.772
The representation must match the packet bytes
the model expects,

00:56:11.048 --> 00:56:14.452
and the complete system must report overload,
dropped

00:56:14.465 --> 00:56:16.918
observations and processing failures.

00:56:17.375 --> 00:56:21.269
A neural processing unit accelerates
computations supported by

00:56:21.282 --> 00:56:22.271
its own runtime.

00:56:22.672 --> 00:56:27.092
Net Mamba Plus currently relies on custom CUDA
operations in the

00:56:27.092 --> 00:56:28.544
checked native implementation.

00:56:28.957 --> 00:56:32.501
A future port needs a chosen target, supported
equivalents for

00:56:32.501 --> 00:56:36.483
the relevant operations, compatible layouts and
an explicit

00:56:36.495 --> 00:56:37.435
precision policy.

00:56:37.835 --> 00:56:41.542
Saving parameter tensors is different from
exporting an

00:56:41.642 --> 00:56:43.407
executable computation graph.

00:56:43.808 --> 00:56:47.940
Both may be necessary, and neither alone proves
the port works.

00:56:48.341 --> 00:56:51.408
Begin by comparing the target&#x27;s model outputs
with the retained

00:56:51.421 --> 00:56:52.523
GPU reference.

00:56:52.936 --> 00:56:56.843
Preserve differences and determine whether they
change decisions

00:56:56.918 --> 00:56:58.934
under the intended operating conditions.

00:56:59.388 --> 00:57:02.491
Optimizations should answer a stated deployment
need.

00:57:02.904 --> 00:57:07.020
Larger batches may improve throughput while
increasing how long a

00:57:07.033 --> 00:57:07.909
packet waits.

00:57:08.297 --> 00:57:12.301
Lower precision can save memory or computation
while changing

00:57:12.301 --> 00:57:14.903
scores and possibly classifications.

00:57:15.354 --> 00:57:19.120
Faster arithmetic does not automatically make
the detector more

00:57:19.133 --> 00:57:19.658
useful.

00:57:20.059 --> 00:57:23.812
A target evaluation should include accuracy on
suitable data,

00:57:24.338 --> 00:57:28.555
latency distributions, throughput, memory use
and dropped work.

00:57:28.955 --> 00:57:33.034
The existing strict score-comparison failures
already show why

00:57:33.047 --> 00:57:37.113
class agreement and identical numerical outputs
must be reported

00:57:37.113 --> 00:57:37.827
separately.

00:57:38.227 --> 00:57:42.306
A port needs its own evidence instead of
borrowing a successful

00:57:42.319 --> 00:57:43.657
check from another machine.

00:57:44.133 --> 00:57:47.939
The current results come from one model seed,
capped groups and

00:57:47.939 --> 00:57:49.492
these supplied data sources.

00:57:49.917 --> 00:57:53.410
Pretrained exposure to related data is not fully
known.

00:57:53.811 --> 00:57:58.518
Exact duplicate grouping does not prove
independent captures, and

00:57:58.530 --> 00:58:01.572
sparse attack-subtype support limits some
conclusions.

00:58:01.973 --> 00:58:05.353
The project has not established reliable
confidence on customer

00:58:05.366 --> 00:58:08.921
traffic, calibrated packet probabilities,
unknown-attack

00:58:08.934 --> 00:58:12.852
coverage, operational alert thresholds or live
capture.

00:58:13.265 --> 00:58:16.395
It also has not run on an N P U or Smart N I

00:58:16.395 --> 00:58:16.808
C.

00:58:17.196 --> 00:58:20.526
These are concrete next tests and engineering
tasks.

00:58:20.927 --> 00:58:24.319
They do not erase the completed training, but
they constrain what

00:58:24.319 --> 00:58:25.045
it proves.

00:58:25.513 --> 00:58:27.441
Here is a defensible explanation.

00:58:27.854 --> 00:58:32.123
We adapted the original Net Mamba Plus encoder
to packet payloads

00:58:32.123 --> 00:58:34.176
from both supplied CSV files.

00:58:34.564 --> 00:58:38.945
We validated the inputs, grouped exact duplicate
payloads across

00:58:38.945 --> 00:58:42.450
sources, trained the declared comparison models
and selected

00:58:42.450 --> 00:58:44.341
checkpoints using validation data.

00:58:44.741 --> 00:58:47.946
We measured the joint model on held-out packet
groups and

00:58:47.946 --> 00:58:49.736
compared it with simpler controls.

00:58:50.149 --> 00:58:54.206
We executed saved-checkpoint inference and
retained both class

00:58:54.206 --> 00:58:56.121
agreement and numerical differences.

00:58:56.522 --> 00:59:00.465
The client repository delivers the executable
workflow; the

00:59:00.465 --> 00:59:04.433
reproduction repository explains the code,
evidence and

00:59:04.433 --> 00:59:05.272
limitations.

00:59:05.673 --> 00:59:09.428
This is a tested research implementation, with
further work

00:59:09.441 --> 00:59:10.542
required for deployment.

